## [ISSUE] `join()` stops terminating flusher processes once the deadline passes

**Severity:** LOW — after a timed-out join, stray flusher processes can still be running when the next strategy starts after a rebalance.

### The Problem

```python
for process_index, process in self.processes.items():
    if deadline is not None:
        remaining_time = deadline - time.time()
        if remaining_time <= 0:
            break

    while process.is_alive() and (deadline is None or deadline > time.time()):
        time.sleep(0.1)

    if isinstance(process, multiprocessing.Process):
        process.terminate()
```

If process 0 uses up the whole timeout, the `break` skips processes 1..N-1, and their terminate branch is never reached. The old single-process code always reached the terminate call after the wait loop.

### Theory / Proof

Arroyo calls `join(timeout)` on partition revocation, then calls `create_with_partitions` for the new assignment. That builds a new `SpanFlusher` with new processes. The old processes see `stopped=True` only at the top of their `while` loop, so one that is in the middle of a long `flush_segments` / produce keeps working on shards that may now belong to another consumer. On top of that, the `isinstance(process, multiprocessing.Process)` check is always False for spawn-context processes (see the comment on line 254), so `terminate()` does not run even for the processes the loop does reach.

### What happens today vs. what will break

With one process (the current CLI default) nothing changes. With N processes and a slow shard, the processes after the slow one get no termination at all.

### Research

The usual pattern is to wait on all workers against one shared deadline, then terminate every one that is still alive, as `concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor.shutdown` and `multiprocessing.Pool.terminate` do.

### Recommendation

```python
for process in self.processes.values():
    while process.is_alive() and (deadline is None or deadline > time.time()):
        time.sleep(0.1)

for process in self.processes.values():
    if isinstance(process, multiprocessing.process.BaseProcess) and process.is_alive():
        process.terminate()
```
